---
// Location page (DESIGN_BRIEF.md section 4). Same standing as /cincinnati:
// exists for search, lives in the footer rather than the main nav, and does
// not conflict with the no-geography project-naming rule — section 4 says so
// explicitly. Nothing here names a project, a client, or an address.
//
// Keyword target (8.3): "custom drapery Northern Kentucky" — title, H1, first
// paragraph, meta description, once each.
//
// Neighbourhoods (8.4): the Kentucky-side names from the brief's list — Fort
// Mitchell, Fort Thomas, Villa Hills, Crestview Hills, Union, Edgewood. The
// Ohio-side names are on /cincinnati. Splitting them is what keeps these two
// pages genuinely different documents rather than the thin near-duplicates
// 8.4 warns against.
//
// Fort Wright is named here because it is where Amy actually is
// (business.ts's `baseCity`) — the one geographic fact about the business
// itself, as distinct from an address, which is never published.
//
// Vendor naming: rule 8's "the lines I represent." No pricing (rule 1).
// Image: standing IMAGE-MANIFEST.md placeholder rule. Swap when Kelsee's
// shoot lands.

import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RevealImage from '../components/RevealImage.astro';
import { photos } from '../data/photos';

const lead = photos['pleated-drapery'];
---

<Base
  title="Custom Drapery Northern Kentucky | Amy K Clark Design"
  description="Custom drapery in Northern Kentucky, plus blinds, shades, upholstery, and soft furnishings. Designed, sewn, and installed by Amy Clark from Fort Wright."
  ogImage="/images/og/og-default.jpg"
  transparentNav={false}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { name: 'Northern Kentucky', href: '/northern-kentucky' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="section wrap text-page__intro">
    <p class="type-label text-page__eyebrow">Service area</p>
    <h1 class="type-display text-page__heading">Custom Drapery in Northern Kentucky</h1>
    <p class="text-page__lead">
      Custom drapery in Northern Kentucky, made a few minutes from most of it. I work out of Fort
      Wright, which means the consultation, the sewing, and the installation are all the same
      person driving the same short distance.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RevealImage
    src={lead.image}
    alt={lead.alt}
    blurDataURL={lead.blurDataURL}
    aspectRatio="16/9"
    sizes="100vw"
  />

  <section class="section wrap text-page__body">
    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">Close enough to come back</h2>
    <p>
      Fort Mitchell, Fort Thomas, Villa Hills, Crestview Hills, Edgewood, and Union are all short
      drives, and that changes what is practical. If a panel needs dressing again a week after it
      hangs, I dress it again. I can drop a memo off to sit in your room overnight so you see it in
      the morning light instead of deciding on the spot. Being close is not really a selling point.
      It is the reason the finishing actually happens.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">Drapery first, then everything around it</h2>
    <p>
      Drapery is the largest piece of fabric in most rooms and it sets the terms for the rest.
      Where the rod goes, how far the panels stack back, what the lining does to the light. Once
      that is settled, blinds and shades, upholstery, and the pillows and cushions can be worked
      around it rather than negotiated against it afterward.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fabric comes from the lines I represent, chosen for how it will hang in your specific room
      rather than how it reads in a book.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">River-view rooms and new builds</h2>
    <p>
      A Fort Thomas room facing the river has a view worth keeping and afternoon light worth
      controlling, which are competing requirements, and it is the reason openness factor is a real
      number rather than a preference. A new build in Union has square openings and no history,
      which makes the work easier and makes the fabric decision carry more of the weight.
    </p>

    <h2 class="type-display text-page__subhead">Start with the room</h2>
    <p>
      Every project starts in your house. See <a href="/services/drapery" class="link-underline"
        >custom drapery</a
      > for how that work gets built, or the <a href="/process" class="link-underline">process</a> for
      what happens in what order.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap contact-band bg-ground">
    <p class="type-display contact-band__line">Ready to start your project?</p>
    <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-solid">Schedule a Consultation</a>
  </section>
</Base>
